"id":"iterative_dfs_true_order",
"source":"iterative_dfs_true_order.py",
"code":"import sys\n\n\ndef main() -> None:\n    raw = sys.stdin.read()\n    data = raw.split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    pos = 0\n    n = int(data[pos])\n    m = int(data[pos + 1])\n    pos += 2\n\n    adj = [\n        [] for _ in range(n)\n    ]\n    for _ in range(m):\n        u = int(data[pos])\n        v = int(data[pos + 1])\n        pos += 2\n        adj[u].append(v)\n        adj[v].append(u)\n\n    seen = [False] * n\n    par = [-1] * n\n    order = [0]\n    seen[0] = True\n    st = [[0, 0]]\n\n    while st:\n        fr = st[-1]\n        node = fr[0]\n        idx = fr[1]\n        deg = len(adj[node])\n        if idx == deg:\n            st.pop()\n            continue\n        nb = adj[node][idx]\n        fr[1] = idx + 1\n        if not seen[nb]:\n            seen[nb] = True\n            par[nb] = node\n            order.append(nb)\n            st.append([nb, 0])\n\n    print(\"Preorder:\", order)\n    print(\"Parent:\", par)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n",
"alt":"The program reads a vertex count, an edge count and that many edges, then walks the tree with one frame per vertex on its own stack: each frame's index advances through that vertex's neighbor list one at a time, only descending into a neighbor the first time it is unvisited, so every vertex gets exactly one parent.",
